Make short immutable strings immortal by interning them, replacing the experiment in 0125.

WHAT WAS WRONG. On macOS a short ASCII string is a TAGGED POINTER: no allocation, nothing to
deallocate, immortal. Applications rely on that without knowing they do. Swift Publisher hands six
character strings to -addObserver:forKeyPath:options:context: and never retains them, which is
correct on a Mac and fatal here, because we allocated a real object and the pool freed it. Clicking
the page in its document editor killed the application 4 of 4.

WHAT THIS DOES. It reproduces the LIFETIME rather than the representation. A short, ASCII,
inline, default allocator immutable string is looked up in a content keyed CFSet on the way out of
the creation funnel. The set retains its members and never removes one, so every member is immortal
and equal strings are shared. Cost is bounded by DISTINCT short strings, not by how many are made.
Tagged pointers would be the faithful fix, but they touch pointer encoding, the objc runtime and
every place that assumes a pointer is dereferenceable; this buys the same safety for this class of
bug at a fraction of the risk.

WHICH STRINGS ARE EXCLUDED, and why. Inline contents only: a string that points at bytes it does
not own would, once immortal, outlive the buffer it was handed, which is a promise its caller never
made. Non default allocators are left alone so nothing is held in an allocator past its zone.
Unicode and anything over nine characters never qualifies, which matches the macOS criterion.

WHAT IT COSTS, measured in the guest with tests/foundation/bench_string_intern.c, 400000 creates,
three runs interned against two runs not, wall clock:
    strings too long to qualify     0.115 -> 0.116 s    no measurable change, and this is the control
    short, already interned         0.111 -> 0.151 s    +100 ns per create, 1.36x
    short, all 400000 distinct      0.110 -> 0.429 s    +800 ns per create, 3.9x
The last line is the honest downside: a workload that creates unbounded DISTINCT short strings pays
about four times per create and grows the table. Memory on three real applications moved by single
digit megabytes with a cruder version of this that did not even share duplicates.

THE A/B IS KEPT. CIDER_IMMORTAL_SHORT_STRINGS overrides the length and 0 turns interning off
entirely, which re-validates the diagnosis in both directions: on, the editor survives the click and
renders; off, the same build dies and captures black.

Task #249.

--- a/CFString.c
+++ b/CFString.c
@@ -1244,6 +1244,73 @@
    !!! Various flags should be combined to reduce number of arguments, if possible
 */
 
+/* On macOS a short ASCII string is a tagged pointer, so it never deallocates, and applications
+ * register one somewhere and never retain it. Swift Publisher does exactly that with a KVO context.
+ * We have no tagged pointers, so reproduce the LIFETIME rather than the representation: intern such
+ * a string in a content keyed set that retains its members and never drops one. Cost is bounded by
+ * DISTINCT short strings, not by how many are created. Task #249.
+ */
+#define __kCFCiderInternedStringMax 9	/* what macOS can hold in a tagged ASCII pointer */
+
+static CFMutableSetRef __CFCiderInternedStrings = NULL;
+static CFLock_t __CFCiderInternLock = CFLockInit;
+
+/* Returns +1, like the funnel it serves. Returns str itself when the string does not qualify. */
+static CFStringRef __CFCiderInternShortString(CFStringRef str) {
+    static int max = -1;
+    /* A string created while we hold the lock would deadlock it, and during CF's own start up the
+     * set cannot be built at all. Both are the same guard. */
+    static __thread Boolean interning = false;
+    const uint8_t *contents;
+    CFIndex length;
+    CFStringRef found;
+
+    if (max < 0) {
+        const char *value = getenv("CIDER_IMMORTAL_SHORT_STRINGS");
+
+        max = (value != NULL && value[0] != '\0') ? atoi(value) : __kCFCiderInternedStringMax;
+    }
+    if (max <= 0 || interning) return str;
+
+    /* Inline contents only: a string pointing at bytes it does not own would, once immortal, hold
+     * a promise the caller never made, and outlive the buffer it was handed. */
+    if (!__CFStrIsEightBit(str) || !__CFStrIsInline(str)) return str;
+    if (__CFGetAllocator(str) != __CFGetDefaultAllocator()) return str;
+
+    length = __CFStrLength(str);
+    if (length < 1 || length > max) return str;
+
+    contents = (const uint8_t *)__CFStrContents(str) + __CFStrSkipAnyLengthByte(str);
+    if (!__CFBytesInASCII(contents, length)) return str;
+
+    interning = true;
+
+    if (__CFCiderInternedStrings == NULL) {
+        CFMutableSetRef table = CFSetCreateMutable(kCFAllocatorSystemDefault, 0, &kCFTypeSetCallBacks);
+
+        __CFLock(&__CFCiderInternLock);
+        if (__CFCiderInternedStrings == NULL) __CFCiderInternedStrings = table;
+        __CFUnlock(&__CFCiderInternLock);
+        if (__CFCiderInternedStrings != table) CFRelease(table);
+    }
+
+    __CFLock(&__CFCiderInternLock);
+    found = (CFStringRef)CFSetGetValue(__CFCiderInternedStrings, str);
+    if (found == NULL) {
+        CFSetAddValue(__CFCiderInternedStrings, str);
+        found = str;
+    }
+    __CFUnlock(&__CFCiderInternLock);
+
+    interning = false;
+
+    if (found != str) {
+        CFRetain(found);
+        CFRelease(str);
+    }
+    return found;
+}
+
 CF_PRIVATE CFStringRef __CFStringCreateImmutableFunnel3(
                         CFAllocatorRef alloc, const void *bytes, CFIndex numBytes, CFStringEncoding encoding,
                         Boolean possiblyExternalFormat, Boolean tryToReduceUnicode, Boolean hasLengthByte, Boolean hasNullByte, Boolean noCopy,
